<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Debug-only overlay.

    The self-hosted LiveKit demo server is a bare GCE IP with no domain, so signalling runs
    over ws:// and needs a cleartext exception. That exception is scoped two ways:

      1. To one host. @xml/network_security_config is generated by
         :app:generateLivekitNetworkConfig from LIVEKIT_URL in the repository-root .env and
         permits cleartext for that single address; the base-config still refuses it
         everywhere else.
      2. To this build type. The release manifest declares no networkSecurityConfig at all
         and keeps android:usesCleartextTraffic="false", so a release APK cannot be talked
         into plaintext by editing a .env.

    WebRTC media is SRTP-encrypted regardless of how the peers were introduced, so what is
    actually exposed here is the signalling channel — the join token, room name, and
    participant identities. Acceptable for a demo on a throwaway VM. Not acceptable in
    production, where the fix is a domain, a certificate, and wss://.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <application android:networkSecurityConfig="@xml/network_security_config" />

</manifest>
